WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.878
<v RAY>[matter-of-fact] Here's a number about
what people actually do with used sharps.

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:07.805
<v RAY>In one study, more than ninety percent of
workers surveyed said they always use a

00:00:07.805 --> 00:00:08.640
<v RAY>sharps container.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.640
<v DANA>[surprised] ALWAYS? To be honest, I'd
have guessed about half.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.667
<v DANA>When I think of a busy line, the
container's always a long walk away. So I

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.560
<v DANA>picture people tossing blades in the
trash.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.735
<v RAY>[dry] That's the version people tend to
tell each other. But MOST people walk it

00:00:24.735 --> 00:00:25.920
<v RAY>over to the container.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.613
<v RAY>Imagine someone on the line finishes a
blade change, so they walk the used blade

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.920
<v RAY>down to the container.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.360
<v DANA>And then what happens?

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.920
<v RAY>They drop it in and walk back to work,
already thinking about the next job.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.520
<v DANA>Then why does it feel like everyone uses
the trash?

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.001
<v RAY>Because tossing it in the trash is the
one you remember, and it's the one people

00:00:46.001 --> 00:00:47.040
<v RAY>bring up at lunch.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.528
<v RAY>You know, nobody ever tells a story about
walking to the container, because it's

00:00:51.528 --> 00:00:52.800
<v RAY>too normal to mention.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.280
<v DANA>So the trash gets all the attention.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.400
<v RAY>It does. And the walk to the container is
just what most people do on a normal day.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.160
<v DANA>Next time I'm walking one down, I won't
feel like the odd one out.

